   "t": 304.431,
   "kind": "text",
   "text": "`/orders` now requires an API key. `GET /orders` and `POST /orders` return 401 unless the `x-api-key` header matches one of the keys in the `API_KEYS` env var, and `/users` stays public. All 13 tests pass. Nothing is committed.\n\n**How it works**\n- **The key check:** `src/auth.js` (new) provides `requireApiKey(handler)`. Keys are a comma-separated list in `API_KEYS`, read on every request, so you can rotate them without a restart. If `API_KEYS` is unset or empty, every `/orders` request is rejected. The comparison takes the same time whether a key is close or not.\n- **Order of checks:** `src/server.js` checks the key before it reads or parses the request body. A request with no key gets a 401, even if its body is invalid JSON.\n- **The routes:** both handlers in `src/routes/orders.js` are wrapped. A wrong or missing key gets `{\"error\":\"unauthorized\"}`.\n- **README:** has a new Auth section, including that keys are trimmed and can't contain commas.\n\n**Who did what**\n\n| Step | Owner | Model |\n|---|---|---|\n| Auth module, wiring the routes and server, README, final review of the diff | Lead (main session) | Opus 5.5 |\n| `test/orders.test.js` (11 tests: missing, wrong, empty, multiple and no keys, body not parsed before auth, `/users` still open) | `squad-sonnet` | Sonnet |\n| Security review: ran the server and sent real requests (header case, duplicate headers, query string, trailing slash, absolute URL, HEAD, `API_KEYS` unset or empty) | `squad-opus` | Opus |\n| Follow-ups from the review: a test that fails if any `/orders` route isn't wrapped, and the README note | Lead | Opus 5.5 |\n\n**Verification**\n```\nnode --test test/*.test.js\n# tests 13\n# pass 13\n# fail 0\n```\nRun the tests with `node --test test/*.test.js`, not `npm test`. On this machine `npm` resolves to the Windows install (`a different npm install`), which runs `node --test` against the wrong folder and fails. That's a setup problem on this machine, not in the repo.\n\n**Open items**\n- **Low risk, from the review:** protection is opt-in per route. The new test catches any unwrapped route in `orders.js`, but a route added in another file wouldn't be covered. If you want protection to be the default, I can protect everything under `/orders` in `server.js` instead.\n- **Already there before this change:** every error returns 400 with the error message, so malformed JSON shows the parser's error text. Request bodies also have no size limit, and `/users` is still open to that.\n- I didn't use Fable, as planned. The Opus review covered the security side."
